Mo off"},{"n":"Portland Discovery Land and Sea Tours","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":43.655746,"lo":-70.252418,"d":356,"w":"https://www.portlanddiscovery.com/","ph":"+1-207-774-0808"},{"n":"Fore River Local Artist Collective","g":"sights","k":"gallery","la":43.656387,"lo":-70.253687,"d":459},{"n":"Tug Stamford Propeller","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":43.660023,"lo":-70.245939,"d":477},{"n":"Maine Narrow Gauge Railroad Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":43.660764,"lo":-70.245694,"d":561,"wp":"en:Maine Narrow Gauge Railroad Museum","wd":"Q1885606","ad":"14 Ocean Gateway Pier","ds":"The Maine Narrow Gauge Railroad Co. & Museum (reporting mark MNGX) is a 2 ft (610 mm) narrow gauge railway, located in Portland, Maine, United States.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Maine-00598_-_Narrow_Gauge_Train_Station.jpg/960px-Maine-00598_-_Narrow_Gauge_Train_Station.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"Maine Narrow Gauge Railroad Museum"},{"n":"The Maine Lobsterman","g":"sights","k":"monument","la":43.657252,"lo":-70.256107,"d":666,"ds":"The Maine Lobsterman is a bronze statue in Lobsterman Park, Portland, Maine, United States. Standing at the intersection of Middle Street and Temple Street, it serves as a monument to the state's fishing industry and its workers. The original sculpture was created by Victor Kahill for the 1939 New York World's Fair and served as the centerpiece of the Maine exhibit in the Hall of States.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/The_Maine_Lobsterman_1_Temple_Street_Portland_ME_August_2023.jpg/960px-The_Maine_Lobsterman_1_Temple_Street_Portland_ME_August_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"The Maine Lobsterman"},{"n":"Canopy Portland Waterfront","g":"sights","k":"hotel","la":43.65407,"lo":-70.25609,"d":685,"w":"https://fathomcompanies.com/canopy-portland-waterfront/","ad":"9 Center Street","wifi":1},{"n":"Grant Wahlquist Gallery","g":"sights","k":"gallery","la":43.657116,"lo":-70.257889,"d":805,"h":"We, Th, Sa 11:00-18:00","w":"https://www.grantwahlquist.com/","ph":"+1-207-245-5732","ad":"30, 2Fl City Center"},{"n":"Maine Jewish Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":43.662536,"lo":-70.25303,"d":831,"w":"https://mainejewishmuseum.org/","ad":"267 Congress Street"},{"n":"Wadsworth-Longfellow House","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":43.656971,"lo":-70.260212,"d":988,"h":"Jun-Oct Tu-Sa 10:00-17:00","w":"https://www.mainehistory.org/","wd":"Q7959331","ad":"483 Congress Street"},{"n":"Maine Historical Society","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":43.65685,"lo":-70.260355,"d":998,"h":"We-Sa 10:00-17:00","w":"https://www.mainehistory.org/","ad":"489 Congress Street"},{"n":"Portland Observatory","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":43.665377,"lo":-70.248252,"d":1042,"w":"https://www.portlandlandmarks.org/observatory/","ph":"+1-207-774-5561","wp":"en:Portland Observatory","wd":"Q2105180","ad":"138 Congress Street","ds":"The Portland Observatory is a historic maritime signal tower at 138 Congress Street in the Munjoy Hill section of Portland, Maine. Built in 1807, it is the only known surviving tower of its type in the United States. Using both a telescope and signal flags, two-way communication between ship and shore was possible several hours before an incoming vessel reached the docks.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Portland_Observatory_2024.jpg/960px-Portland_Observatory_2024.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"Portland Observatory"},{"n":"Bug Light","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":43.65554,"lo":-70.234853,"d":1058,"wd":"Q7231886"},{"n":"SPACE Gallery","g":"sights","k":"gallery","la":43.655668,"lo":-70.26126,"d":1067,"ad":"534 Congress Street"},{"n":"Victoria Mansion","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":43.651534,"lo":-70.260747,"d":1139,"w":"https://www.victoriamansion.org/","wp":"en:Victoria Mansion","wd":"Q7926852","ad":"109 Danforth Street","ds":"Victoria Mansion, also known as the Morse-Libby House or Morse-Libby Mansion, is a historic house in downtown Portland, Maine, United States. The brownstone exterior, elaborate interior design, opulent furnishings and early technological conveniences provide a detailed portrait of lavish living in nineteenth-century America.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Victoria_Mansion%2C_Portland%2C_Maine_USA.jpg/960px-Victoria_Mansion%2C_Portland%2C_Maine_USA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"Victoria Mansion"},{"n":"Cushing's Point Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":43.652379,"lo":-70.234316,"d":1172,"w":"https://www.sphistory.org/","ph":"+1 207-676-7299","ad":"55"},{"n":"Portland Museum of Art","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":43.653738,"lo":-70.26227,"d":1175,"h":"Tu-Th 10:00-18:00, Fr 10:00-20:00, Sa-Su 10:00-18:00, Th[3] 10:00-20:00","w":"https://www.portlandmuseum.org/","ph":"+1-207-775-6148","wp":"en:Portland Museum of Art","wd":"Q2105170","ad":"7 Congress Square","ds":"The Portland Museum of Art, or PMA, is the largest and oldest public art institution in Maine. Founded as the Portland Society of Art in 1882. It is located in the downtown area known as The Arts District in Portland, Maine.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/McLellan_House%2C_Portland_ME.jpg/960px-McLellan_House%2C_Portland_ME.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"Portland Museum of Art"},{"n":"Mast of the USS Portland","g":"sights","k":"monument","la":43.665781,"lo":-70.240043,"d":1261,"wp":"en:USS Portland (CA-33)","wd":"Q2448527","ds":"USS Portland (CL/CA–33) was the lead ship of the Portland class of cruiser and the first ship of the United States Navy named after the city of Portland, Maine.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/USS_Portland_%28CA-33%29_at_Pearl_Harbor_1942.jpg/960px-USS_Portland_%28CA-33%29_at_Pearl_Harbor_1942.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"USS Portland (CA-33)"},{"n":"Fort Sumner Park","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":43.667256,"lo":-70.250928,"d":1273},{"n":"Portland Fire Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":43.651944,"lo":-70.263892,"d":1355,"wp":"en:Portland Fire Museum","wd":"Q7231924","ds":"The Portland Fire Museum is a fire museum in Portland, Maine. Located at 157 Spring Street in the former home of Fire Engine 4, the museum is operated by the Portland Veteran Firemen's Association (PVFA). It showcases the history of firefighting in Portland, including a number of retired firetrucks.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Portland_Fire_Museum.jpg/960px-Portland_Fire_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"Portland Fire Museum"},{"n":"First Civic Monument","g":"sights","k":"monument","la":43.668973,"lo":-70.244131,"d":1475},{"n":"East End Beach","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":43.668255,"lo":-70.240248,"d":1498},{"n":"Neal Dow House","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":43.652789,"lo":-70.269054,"d":1731,"wp":"en:Neal Dow House","wd":"Q6984184","ad":"714 Congress Street","ds":"The Neal Dow House, also known as Gen. Neal Dow House, is a historic house found at 714 Congress Street in Portland, Maine. It was built in 1829 for noted politician and prohibitionist Neal Dow (1804–1897), and was later designated a National Historic Landmark for that association.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/DowHouse.JPG/960px-DowHouse.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"Neal Dow House"},{"n":"Fort Preble","g":"sights","k":"fort","la":43.649125,"lo":-70.225914,"d":1934},{"n":"Willard Beach","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":43.643493,"lo":-70.226052,"d":2247,"wd":"Q33346658"},{"n":"Landing Beach","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":43.66374,"lo":-70.22