,"d":1508,"ph":"+1-207-871-8519","ad":"125 Forest Avenue"},{"n":"Bagheera (schooner)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.655833,"lo":-70.247778,"d":25,"ds":"Bagheera, formerly Beacon Rock, is a historic schooner normally berthed at the Maine State Pier in Portland, Maine. She is a two-masted auxiliary rigged schooner, built in 1924 by the noted naval architect John G. Alden, and is noted for her racing success on the Great Lakes. She is now owned and operated by the Portland Schooner Company, which offers sailing tours of Casco Bay, using Bagheera and Wendameen.","wpu":"Bagheera (schooner)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/BagheeraSchooner.jpg/960px-BagheeraSchooner.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Maine State Pier","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.6571,"lo":-70.2482,"d":123,"ds":"The Maine State Pier is a municipal-owned deepwater marine facility and music venue located at the intersection of Commercial Street and Franklin Street on the eastern waterfront in Portland, Maine. Located to the north of Maine Wharf, it was completed in 1924.","wpu":"Maine State Pier","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Maine_State_Pier_2022.jpg/960px-Maine_State_Pier_2022.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fort Loyal","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.659,"lo":-70.2481,"d":333,"ds":"Fort Loyal was a British settler refuge and colonial outpost built in 1678 at Falmouth (present-day Portland, Maine) in Casco Bay. It was destroyed in 1690 by Abenaki and French forces at the Battle of Fort Loyal. The fort was rebuilt in 1742 and renamed Falmouth Fort before King George's War and rearmed again in 1755 for the French and Indian War.","wpu":"Fort Loyal","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1e/Fort_Loyal_1763.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"St. Peter's Catholic Church (Portland, Maine)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.660837,"lo":-70.251882,"d":621,"ds":"St. Peter's Catholic Church is a church located on Federal Street in downtown Portland, Maine, United States. Owned by the Roman Catholic Diocese of Portland, the church was built in 1929, and—being in the city's Italian neighborhood centered on India Street—today hosts Italian cultural events, including the annual Italian Bazaar on the feast day of Saint Rocco.","wpu":"St. Peter's Catholic Church (Portland, Maine)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/St._Peter_Parish_Church_2025.jpg/960px-St._Peter_Parish_Church_2025.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Pullen Fountain","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.65903,"lo":-70.2554,"d":684,"ds":"Stanley Thomas Pullen Fountain is an historic public water fountain and horse trough in Portland, Maine. It is named for Stanley T. Pullen, a former president of the Maine State Society for the Protection of Animals. Pullen died in 1910; the fountain's installation date is not known.","wpu":"Pullen Fountain","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/PullenFountain.jpg/960px-PullenFountain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Lincoln Park (Portland, Maine)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.659722,"lo":-70.255,"d":698,"ds":"Lincoln Park is a 1.8-acre (0.73 ha) urban park in downtown Portland, Maine. Created as Phoenix Square in 1866, following that year’s Great Fire, which burned down most of the buildings of Portland, it was renamed on January 24, 1867, in honor of former president Abraham Lincoln. It is bounded by Congress Street to the west, Pearl Street to the south, Federal Street to the east and Franklin Street to the north.","wpu":"Lincoln Park (Portland, Maine)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/LincolnParkMaine.JPG/960px-LincolnParkMaine.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Abyssinian Meeting House","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.6623,"lo":-70.2485,"d":701,"ds":"The Abyssinian Meeting House is a historic church building at 73–75 Newbury Street, in the Munjoy Hill neighborhood of Portland, Maine. Built between 1828 and 1831 by free African-Americans, it is Maine's oldest African-American church building, and the third-oldest in the nation.","wpu":"Abyssinian Meeting House","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Abyssinian_Meeting_House_Portland_Maine_2021.jpg/960px-Abyssinian_Meeting_House_Portland_Maine_2021.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"John Ford Statue","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.654611,"lo":-70.257306,"d":764,"ds":"John Ford Statue, alternatively titled The John Ford Statue or simply John Ford, is a bronze statue of American film director John Ford (1894–1973) sculpted by George Kelly. Located in the historically Irish neighborhood of Gorham's Corner in Portland, Maine, the statue was unveiled in 1998 and funded by Ford's friend, philanthropist Linda Noe Laine.","wpu":"John Ford Statue","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/John_Ford_Statue_Portland.jpg/960px-John_Ford_Statue_Portland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Eastern Cemetery","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.662778,"lo":-70.250833,"d":787,"ds":"Eastern Cemetery is a historic cemetery at the intersection of Washington Avenue and Congress Street in the Munjoy Hill neighborhood of Portland, Maine. Established in 1668, it is the city's oldest historic site. It has more than 4,000 marked graves with an estimated further 3,000 burials in unmarked plots.","wpu":"Eastern Cemetery","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Eastern_Cemetery%2C_Portland.JPG/960px-Eastern_Cemetery%2C_Portland.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Cathedral of the Immaculate Conception (Portland, Maine)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.661389,"lo":-70.254722,"d":807,"ds":"The Cathedral of the Immaculate Conception is a historic cathedral on Cumberland Avenue in Portland, Maine, which serves as seat of the Diocese of Portland. The rector is Father Seamus Griesbach. The church, an imposing Gothic Revival structure built in 1866–69, was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1985.","wpu":"Cathedral of the Immaculate Conception (Portland, Maine)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Cathedral_of_the_Immaculate_Conception_Portland_ME_2012.jpg/960px-Cathedral_of_the_Immaculate_Conception_Portland_ME_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Merrill Auditorium","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":43.65955,"lo":-70.25725,"d":842,"ds":"Merrill Auditorium is a 1,908-seat auditorium located in Portland, Maine, United States. Originally known as Portland City Hall Auditorium, it is located in the eastern section of Portland City Hall. The auditorium was built in 1912 and underwent a major rebuild and renovation in 1997.","wpu":"Merrill Auditorium","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Merrill_Auditorium_2025.jpg/960px-Merrill_Auditorium_2025.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Portland is the most populous city in the U.S. state of Maine. Its population was 68,408 at the 2020 census and the Greater Portland metropolitan area has a population of approximately 550,000 people, the most populous metropolitan area in Maine. It is the county seat of Cumberland County.","wpu":"Portland, Maine","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/TheOldPort.jpg/960px-TheOldPort.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}